Title: Hoping to Win, Expected to Lose: Theory and Lessons on Microenterprise Development
Abstract: We show how financial and managerial constraints impede experimentation and thus limit learning
about the profitability of investments. Imperfect information about one’s own type, but willingness
to experiment to learn one’s type, leads to short-run negative expected returns to investments, with
some outliers succeeding. We find in an experiment that entrepreneurs invest randomized grants of
cash and adopt advice from randomized grants of consulting services, but both lead to lower profits
on average. In the long run, they revert back to their prior scale of operations. In a meta-analysis,
results from 19 other experiments find mixed support for this theory.
